## Visual direction gate — two directions ready for selection

**First, an honest capability finding.** There is no callable image generator in this session (no MCP servers, no generation tool in the tool list, no API credentials; Pexels returns 401). Per the Dreative skill's stated fallback, both mockups are **material-backed page compositions authored in HTML/SVG/canvas and rendered by headless Chromium** at 1440px and 390px — real, inspectable PNGs, not prompts and not diffusion output. The precise probe results are in `design-blocker.md`.

**Second, a material finding that shaped both directions.** A 24-query sweep across Openverse/Wikimedia/rawpixel/StockSnap (contact sheet: `design/_src/contact.png`) found **no coherent modern-menswear product photography** — but good fabric macro photography. So both directions make the same disclosed decision: *the real photograph is the cloth; the garment form is authored vector art.* Nine duotone cloth plates were produced from five CC0/CC-BY/CC-BY-SA sources and toned to each garment's stated colour so they read as one shoot (`design/SOURCES.md`). A Harris Tweed shot was pulled because it carried a third-party certification mark.

### Direction A — The Bolt
One unbroken column of cloth from hero to footer; every garment is a knockout cut through it showing its own fabric at macro scale. The focal mechanism is a single one: a **scroll-driven canvas quantisation** that takes the weave from 2px blocks to ~120px tiles, whose last row resolves into the product grid's columns. The idea is still operating inside the buying task — a card is another cut in the same cloth, not a container for a picture.

### Direction B — The Cutting Table
Paper ground, grid, blueprint blue. Opens on a lay plan of the Chore Jacket's pattern pieces inside a marked cloth width; scroll converges them into the flat, which becomes the first product tile. Garments are numbered drawings, facts are numbered clauses, the footer is a drawing title block. Lighter to build, far more legible — but the cloth survives only as a 54px swatch.

**I recommend Direction A**, with the reason stated in its plan. B's load-bearing weakness is visible in `design/b-2-assembly.png`: pattern pieces don't naturally tile into a silhouette (the sleeves still don't reach the shoulders at frame 04), and making them land would mean drafting nine geometrically true lay plans. B also gives the buyer a weaker view of what they're spending £210 on. I've left B fully argued rather than strawmanned — it's the better choice if you want an information-led, paper-white shop, and a combination is viable.

